  <Document>
    <name>🌡️ LIVE AIR TEMPERATURE</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 8:32PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 9:32PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE AIR TEMPERATURE</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/air_temperature/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261004-t23z" width="600" alt="legend"><br>2 m air temperature (degF), FIXED key (-60..150 F) with a STEADY gradient on the map: the raster blends smoothly through blue-white extreme cold &rarr; gray-blue subfreezing &rarr; <b>royal-blue freezing wall (30-40)</b> &rarr; frigid turquoise/blues (40-55) &rarr; transitional lime/teals (55-70) &rarr; mellow yellows (70-85) &rarr; warming orange/golds (85-100) &rarr; <b>scorching pink/reds (100+) to HIGHEST+ 150</b>, passing exactly through every key color (key bars show the <b>LOWEST -60</b> to <b>HIGHEST+ 150</b> band centers). Same temperature always shows the same color; every color is brightened so nothing renders dark. Freezing (32 F) sits inside the royal-blue wall. Record <b>LOWEST 27.11</b> / <b>HIGHEST+ 89.09</b>.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m temperature analysis, hourly cycles<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE AIR TEMPERATURE</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 8:32PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 9:32PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE AIR TEMPERATURE</h2><p>Near-surface (2 m) air temperature from the operational HRRR analysis. Banded 5-degree key (-60..150 F): blue-white extreme cold, gray-blue subfreezing, royal-blue freezing wall (30-40), frigid turquoise/blues (40-55), lime/teal transition (55-70), mellow yellows (70-85), orange/gold warming (85-100), scorching pink/reds past 100. Every band brightened so nothing renders dark.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/air_temperature/legend.png?v=hrrr-hrrr.20261004-t23z" width="600" alt="key"></p><p>2 m air temperature (degF), FIXED key (-60..150 F) with a STEADY gradient on the map: the raster blends smoothly through blue-white extreme cold &rarr; gray-blue subfreezing &rarr; <b>royal-blue freezing wall (30-40)</b> &rarr; frigid turquoise/blues (40-55) &rarr; transitional lime/teals (55-70) &rarr; mellow yellows (70-85) &rarr; warming orange/golds (85-100) &rarr; <b>scorching pink/reds (100+) to HIGHEST+ 150</b>, passing exactly through every key color (key bars show the <b>LOWEST -60</b> to <b>HIGHEST+ 150</b> band centers). Same temperature always shows the same color; every color is brightened so nothing renders dark. Freezing (32 F) sits inside the royal-blue wall. Record <b>LOWEST 27.11</b> / <b>HIGHEST+ 89.09</b>.</p><p><b>Units:</b> degF (source Kelvin)<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP HRRR 3 km 2 m temperature analysis, hourly cycles<br/><b>Update:</b> hourly analysis cycles<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/cgi-bin/filter_hrrr_2d.pl">NOMADS HRRR</a></p>]]></description>
